            "• The 'if' statement executes a block of code only when a condition is True.\n"
            "• The 'else' statement provides an alternative action when the 'if' condition is False.\n\n"
            "[Example Code]\n"
            "  score = 65\n"
            "  if score >= 75:\n"
            "      print(\"Passed\")\n"
            "  else:\n"
            "      print(\"Failed\")\n"
            "-> Output: Failed (since 65 is less than 75, the else block runs)."
        ),
    },
    7: {
        "title": "Lesson 7: The if-elif-else Statement",
        "content": (
            "• Used when there are multiple conditions to evaluate.\n"
            "• Python checks conditions from top to bottom and executes the first True condition.\n\n"
            "[Example Code]\n"
            "  score = 85\n"
            "  if score >= 90:\n"
            "      print(\"Excellent\")\n"
            "  elif score >= 80:\n"
            "      print(\"Very Good\")  <-- This block executes!\n"
            "  elif score >= 75:\n"
            "      print(\"Passed\")\n"
            "  else:\n"
            "      print(\"Failed\")\n"
            "-> Output: Very Good"
        ),
    },
    8: {
        "title": "Lesson 8: Nested Conditions in Python",
        "content": (
            "• A nested condition is a condition inside another condition.\n"
            "• The inner condition is checked only if the outer condition evaluates to True.\n\n"
            "[Example Structure]\n"
            "  if condition1:\n"
            "      if condition2:\n"
            "          # executed if both are True\n"
            "      else:\n"
            "          # executed if condition1 is True but condition2 is False\n"
            "  else:\n"
            "      # executed if condition1 is False"
        ),
    },
    9: {
        "title": "Lesson 9: Logical Operators (and, or, not)",
        "content": (
            "• Logical operators combine or reverse conditions:\n"
            "  1. 'and': Returns True ONLY when ALL conditions are True.\n"
            "  2. 'or' : Returns True when AT LEAST ONE condition is True.\n"
            "  3. 'not': Reverses the result of a condition (True -> False, False -> True)."
        ),
    },
    10: {
"title": "Lesson 10: Introduction to Iteration (Loops)",
        "content": (
            "• Concept of Iteration:\n"
            "  - Loops allow programs to execute a block of code multiple times without code repetition.\n\n"
            "--- 1. The 'for' Loop ---\n"
            "  - Ideal when the number of iterations is known in advance or when iterating over sequences.\n"
            "  - Using range(start, stop, step):\n"
            "      for i in range(1, 4):\n"
            "          print(f\"Iteration #{i}\")\n"
            "      # Output prints Iteration #1, #2, #3\n\n"
            "--- 2. The 'while' Loop ---\n"
            "  - Continues repeating as long as a target condition remains True.\n"
            "  - Requires a loop control variable update to avoid INFINITE LOOPS.\n"
            "      count = 1\n"
            "      while count <= 3:\n"
            "          print(f\"Loop count: {count}\")\n"
            "          count += 1    # Increment control variable\n\n"
